Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"38195","code":"AD2","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["F"],"start":600,"end":650,"location":"1128 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"38197","code":"AD3","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["F"],"start":660,"end":710,"location":"1128 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"51496","code":"AD4","type":"Discussion/Recitation","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Discussion/Recitation","days":["F"],"start":780,"end":830,"location":"1128 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"38199","code":"AL1","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["M","W"],"start":600,"end":650,"location":"1000 Lincoln Hall","instructors":["Kourtikakis, K"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"PS 300","title":"Special Topics","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"54780","code":"COU","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":630,"end":710,"location":"108 David Kinley Hall","instructors":["Obrien Udry, C"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026. Climate change is less a collective-action problem than a fight over who pays and who profits. This seminar reads treaties, court rulings, and movement claims as political bargains, asking who wins, who loses, and whose voices shaped the law. It covers the UN climate regime, the politics of decarbonization and obstruction, energy transitions, petrostates, small island diplomacy, loss and damage, climate migration, and litigation from Pakistan to the ICJ. Students interview practitioners, expand Wikipedia’s coverage of Global South climate politics, and submit an op-ed or rulemaking comment to a real institution. No legal training required."},{"crn":"57684","code":"NG","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":900,"end":980,"location":"319 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Grossman, N"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026. The Information Revolution is changing the world in all sorts of ways. \"Politics of the internet\" explores how the internet has affected politics—including politicians' public communications, terrorist recruitment, foreign influence operations, campaigning, the structure and behavior of political media, and more—as well as political questions of the internet itself, such as social media content moderation and free speech. Students will be expected to attend and participate in discussions, write multiple papers, and present material to the class. Not available to students who have taken an advanced-level version of this class in SP22, SP23, SP25 or SP26. Not available to students who have taken this course as PS 199 in SP24."},{"crn":"65213","code":"VEA","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["T","R"],"start":570,"end":650,"location":"207 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Erlenbusch-Anderson, V"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026. Title: Frantz Fanon’s Political Thought. Frantz Fanon is best known as a theorist of violence, colonialism, and revolution. This course will focus on his lesser-known writings on freedom, alienation, and psychiatric practice, which illuminate how social and political conditions shape our sense of self and our ways of being in the world. By exploring Fanon’s clinical and political writings, we will examine liberation as a process not only of political decolonization but also of subjective transformation and new forms of life."}]},{"id":"PS 302","title":"The US Constitution II","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"61878","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":570,"end":650,"location":"219 David Kinley Hall","instructors":["Seitz, S"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026."}]},{"id":"PS 305","title":"The US Supreme Court","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"38229","code":"OL1","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"B","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026."}]},{"id":"PS 314","title":"Political Psychology","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"66657","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":630,"end":710,"location":"1002 Lincoln Hall","instructors":["Ksiazkiewicz, A"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026."}]},{"id":"PS 316","title":"Latina/Latino Politics","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"60393","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"103 1207 W Oregon","instructors":["Rodriguez, R"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"PS 321","title":"Principles of Public Policy","creditHours":"3 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"39208","code":"A","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":570,"end":650,"location":"1090 Lincoln Hall","instructors":["Hinchliffe, J"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Political Science major(s).","Not intended for students with Freshman class standing."],"note":"Major restrictions will be lifted by noon on Monday, November 16, 2026. Day1Access course mate